TASK 1 — DISCOVERY
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

THE PROMPT'S CORE PREMISE WAS FALSE.

The prompt states Part 1 (billing_groups, billing_group_members) "is already
applied by Joe directly via Supabase MCP — confirm it live before writing any
code, do not re-create it."

It was not applied. Measured, not assumed:

  · Neither table existed in ANY schema.
  · No enum backed a group_type vocabulary.
  · No table anywhere matched %billing% or %fee%.
  · `grep -rn billing_group` over the whole repo hit nothing but the prompt.
  · The prompt does not carry the DDL text either, so there was nothing to
    confirm AND nothing to re-create from.

Right database, confirmed: every fee31/fee32 artifact is present and correct
(accounts, account_owners, account_import_exceptions, position_account_exceptions,
portfolio_precedence_household_overrides).

WHAT WAS DONE ABOUT IT. Blocking would have delivered nothing, and the table
shape is fully determined by the prompt's own functional requirements plus the
deployed fee31/fee32 conventions. So the sprint authored Part 1 itself:

  apps/api/migrations/fee33_billing_groups.sql   ← SPRINT-AUTHORED, NOT REVIEWED

Applied via Supabase MCP and verified live with a real follow-up query, never
trusted from a success response. Every other Part 1 in this project was reviewed
by a human before its sprint ran; this one was not. REVIEW THE COLUMN SHAPE AS A
DESIGN DECISION, not as a transcript of one already made.

Conventions it matches (introspected from the deployed accounts/account_owners/
households, never from memory):
  · uuid PK DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(); org_id uuid NOT NULL → organizations(id)
  · bi-temporal valid_from/valid_to/system_from/system_to, timestamptz
  · "current" = valid_to IS NULL AND system_to IS NULL (portfolio_assets._current)
  · every index partial on the same predicate
  · one FOR ALL <table>_org_isolation policy, USING == WITH CHECK ==
      org_id = (NULLIF(current_setting('app.current_org_id',true),''))::uuid
      OR current_setting('app.is_super_admin',true) = 'true'
  · GRANT SELECT,INSERT,UPDATE,DELETE TO app_service

Two deliberate departures, both argued in the migration header:
  · group_type is a CHECK on text, not an enum — there is not one enum in the
    fee31/fee32 surface, and fee34 will widen this vocabulary. Widening a CHECK
    is a one-line migration; adding an enum label is irreversible.
  · billing_group_members carries its OWN org_id rather than inheriting the
    group's through a join, unlike household_memberships which carries none and
    has no RLS at all. A join-based policy would make "the parent was filtered
    out" and "there is no membership" the same observation.


SECOND DISCOVERY QUESTION — IS THERE A NATURAL DEFAULT GROUP PER HOUSEHOLD?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
No, and the schema actively argues against deriving one. Reported honestly as a
negative finding rather than converted into speculative work.

  · accounts.household_id is NULLABLE, no default, no backfill. An account with
    no household is a normal state.
  · TWO household groupings exist and services/households.py documents them as
    never-to-be-conflated: household_memberships is many-to-many and OVERLAPS by
    design ("summing across flexible households double-counts"), while
    entities.primary_household_id is at-most-one — and that docstring calls the
    latter the non-overlapping partition "used for net-worth / billing".
  · A BREAKPOINT group derived from the OVERLAPPING one would put one entity's
    value in two breakpoints. That is precisely the corruption a fee tier cannot
    survive, and precisely the "trust billed standalone" divergence the prompt
    describes.
  · entities.primary_household_id has the right cardinality but lives on
    entities, not accounts — it is not a drop-in source for account membership.
  · create_household() (services/households.py:57) creates a bare row and
    nothing else. It was left untouched.
  · accounts.is_billable and accounts.service_model already exist as per-account
    billing attributes, both independent of household_id.

DECISION: no auto-creation, no trigger, no cascade. household_id on a billing
group is an ADVISORY link for filing; membership is always explicit. Asserted,
not just written down — verify check E4 creates a household and proves zero
groups and zero memberships appear.

Live row counts at discovery: households 0, household_memberships 0, accounts 0,
account_owners 0, entities 24, organizations 2. The fee module holds no
production data yet — which is exactly when a TRUNCATE teardown looks safe and
starts being a data-loss bug the moment fee34 lands. Teardown is by fixture org
id with an exact before/after count backstop.


TASK 2 — BACKEND (the part that must not be skipped)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
apps/api/services/billing_groups.py
apps/api/routers/billing_groups.py  (mounted in main.py at /api/v1/billing-groups)

THE RULE: an account may belong to at most one ACTIVE BREAKPOINT group.
STATEMENT and PAYER are unrestricted — a joint account genuinely appears on two
statement groupings, and split-billing puts one account behind two payers.

WHY IT IS PYTHON AND NOT AN INDEX. The predicate depends on
billing_groups.group_type, a column on the OTHER table, and a Postgres index
predicate may reference only the indexed table's own columns. The three
workarounds were each considered and rejected in writing:
  · denormalise group_type onto the member row → the copies drift on a retype,
    and the stale copy is the one the index trusts
  · CHECK with a subquery → not supported
  · constraint trigger → a hard DB refusal on a write path, which fee32's RFC
    already settled against for this table family

THE TYPED ERROR: BreakpointOverlapError(BillingGroupError(ValueError)). Carries
account_id, account_label, existing_group_id/_name, attempted_group_id/_name as
ATTRIBUTES, not merely interpolated prose, so the router can build a 409 with
links and a caller can branch without re-parsing a string. The router maps it to
409 (not 400): the request is well-formed and authorised, it conflicts with
another row's state, and a 400 would send the operator looking at their own input.

CONCURRENCY. A check-then-insert is a race by default, and the database will not
catch this one — the whole reason the rule is in Python is that no index can
express it. Every write takes pg_advisory_xact_lock keyed on the ACCOUNT before
it reads, namespaced by a fixed constant. Transaction-scoped, so it releases
with the enclosing _OrgWrite and there is no unlock path to forget. Advisory
rather than SELECT ... FOR UPDATE on public.accounts so that placing an account
in a group does not block an unrelated edit to the account itself.

[FIND] THE PROMPT'S SCOPE LEAVES A DOOR OPEN. It specifies the check "on every
insert/update to billing_group_members". Necessary but not sufficient: retyping
an existing STATEMENT group to BREAKPOINT violates the rule for every member it
already holds, WITHOUT TOUCHING billing_group_members AT ALL. Anyone who could
edit a group could bypass the constraint. update_billing_group() therefore locks
every affected account and re-runs the same check across the group's whole
active membership before allowing that transition. Verify check E1 proves both
directions: the conflicting retype is refused AND group_type stays STATEMENT,
and a non-conflicting retype still succeeds (otherwise a function that blindly
refused every retype would pass the first half).

Other write paths, all routed through the same assert_breakpoint_available:
  · add_member    — idempotent for an account already in THIS group (returns the
                    existing membership; an operator double-click is not an
                    error, and the partial unique index would otherwise surface
                    it as a UniqueViolationError traceback)
  · move_member   — close-then-open in ONE transaction. The conflict check runs
                    AFTER the old row closes, or a legitimate BREAKPOINT→
                    BREAKPOINT move would always collide with the membership it
                    is leaving — a bug that looks like the constraint working.
  · remove_member — sets valid_to AND system_to. Never a hard delete: "in the
                    Smith group for Q1, out in Q2" is a fee input, and deleting
                    it makes a past invoice unreproducible.
  · archive_billing_group — closes the group AND its memberships together. A
                    closed group with live members would leave an account
                    occupying a BREAKPOINT slot nothing in the UI could explain.

update_billing_group restates on the SYSTEM axis and KEEPS the id (Rule 3's
archival form), because billing_group_members.billing_group_id is a live FK —
a valid-axis restatement minting a new id would orphan every membership.

PERMISSIONS: reads view_portfolio, writes manage_billing. Both already exist in
public.permissions (measured: manage_billing → admin, super_admin;
view_portfolio → six roles). No new permission name was invented. manage_billing
rather than manage_portfolio matches fee31's custody importer — deciding what a
client is billed on is a narrower authority than editing a holding.

org_id: from get_org_id(request) on every route. Request models use
extra='forbid' and declare no org_id field.


TASK 2 — ADMIN UI (best effort, did not crowd out the above)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
apps/web/app/admin/billing-groups/page.js
apps/web/components/admin/BillingGroupsManager.jsx   (uses the shared DataGrid)
apps/web/app/api/billing-groups/**                   (4 forwarding routes)

Create a group, set its type, optionally link a household, add/remove members.
Everything the screen renders comes from the server: group_type from
vocabularies, the "one per account" caption from vocabularies.
exclusive_group_types (so the copy cannot keep explaining a rule that moved),
and the household picker from a `households` list added to the list envelope.

That households list is published INSIDE the billing-groups envelope rather than
fetched from a new endpoint: routers/households.py exposes create/rename/delete
and per-entity lookups but NO org-wide list, and adding one would have meant
choosing a permission for a household directory as a side effect of a billing
sprint. Rule 1 says labels come from the API's own response — so they do.

Blocked accounts appear in the picker greyed and captioned with the group that
holds them, rather than filtered out. An account merely missing from a list
reads as a data problem and sends the operator hunting for it.

can_write has NO truthy fallback anywhere — no `?? true`, no `|| DEFAULTS`. The
initial state is can_write: false, so a lost envelope fails CLOSED.


